li {} [:a {:href "/statistics/medal/country"} "Medals by country"]] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/statistics/medal/athlete"} "Medals by athlete"]] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/statistics/medal_table"} "Medal tables"]] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/statistics/participation"} "Participations"]] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/records"} "Olympic Records"]] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/statistics/age"} "Age records"]] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/statistics/age_at_death"} "Age at death"]] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/lists"} "Lists"]] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/today"} "Today"]] "\n" [:li {}] "\n" [:li {:class "divider"}] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/statistics"} "Statistics"]] "\n"] "\n"] "\n\n\n\n\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/feedbacks/new"} "Feedback"]] "\n" [:form {:class "navbar-form navbar-left", :role "search", :action "/athletes/quick_search", :accept-charset "UTF-8", :method "post"} [:input {:name "utf8", :type "hidden", :value "✓", :autocomplete "off"}] [:input {:type "hidden", :name "authenticity_token", :value "Jx_J-N1YRODUoior4jPxhRxBf4sPgA0q6_nXOz9Ix8tQIPHGzpX8ZerqH26yIHujfU5967GRzzv3Gmo1t-0NfA", :autocomplete "off"}] [:div {:class "form-group"} "\n" [:input {:type "text", :name "query", :id "query", :class "form-control", :placeholder "Athlete search"}] "\n"] "\n" [:input {:type "submit", :name "commit", :value "Go", :class "btn btn-default", :data-disable-with "Go"}] "\n"] "\n"] "\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:div {:class "container"} "\n" [:ol {:class "breadcrumb"} "\n" [:li {} "\n" [:a {:href "/"} "Home"] "\n"] "\n" [:li {} [:a {:href "/editions"} "Games"]] [:li {:class "active"} "1976 Winter Olympics Overview"] "\n"] "\n\n\n" [:h1 {} "1976 Winter Olympics"] "\n" [:h2 {} "Facts"] "\n" [:table {:class "biotable"} "\n" [:tbody {} [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Competition type"] "\n" [:td {} "Olympic Games"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Number and Year"] "\n" [:td {} "XII / 1976"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Host city"] "\n" [:td {} "\nInnsbruck, Austria\n(" [:a {:href "/venues/editions/40"} "Venues"] ")\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Opening ceremony"] "\n" [:td {} "\n 4 February\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Closing ceremony"] "\n" [:td {} "\n15 February\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Competition dates"] "\n" [:td {} "\n2 – 15 February\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "OCOG"] "\n" [:td {} [:a {:href "/organizations/365"} "Denver Olympic Organizing Committee"] ", " [:a {:href "/organizations/366"} "Organisationskomitee für die XII. Olympischen Winterspiele Innsbruck 1976"]] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Participants"] "\n" [:td {} "\n" [:a {:href "/counts/edition/40"} "1129"] "\nfrom " [:a {:href "/editions/40/countries"} "37 countries"] "\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Medal events"] "\n" [:td {} "\n37\nin 10 disciplines\n"] "\n"] "\n"]] "\n\n" [:h2 {} "\nOverview\n\n"] "\n" [:div {:class "description"} "\n" [:p {} "In May 1970, the IOC awarded the 12th Olympic Winter Games to Denver, Colorado – and later rued the choice. The citizens of Denver, probably prompted at least in part by the debacle that was occurring with the wanton spending in Montréal for 1976, did not want the Olympics. They were also very concerned about the environmental impact that would be made by construction of all the sporting facilities. In November 1972, a public referendum voted against the Olympics and on 12 November 1972, Denver informed the IOC that they would not host the 1976 Olympic Winter Games. On short notice, the IOC turned to Innsbruck again, choosing that city over quickly organized bids from Lake Placid (USA), Mont Blanc (FRA), and Tampere (FIN). And once again Innsbruck demonstrated how well an Olympic Winter Games could be staged."] "\n\n" [:p {} "It was lucky for the Winter Olympics that in 1972 " [:a {:href "/athletes/899230"} "Lord Killanin"] " succeeded " [:a {:href "/athletes/78168"} "Avery Brundage"] " as IOC President. Brundage had developed a hatred of the Olympic Winter Games, feeling that they were too commercialized. At his farewell address to the IOC, Brundage stated that he hoped, “the Winter Olympics receive a decent burial in Denver.” Killanin wrote in his autobiography (My Olympic Years) that Brundage was pleased when Denver’s Organizing Committee began to have problems. Killanin himself noted, “I am sure that there will be some people in the IOC who will favor winding up the Winter Olympics altogether.” Fortunately, Innsbruck saved the Winter Olympic idea."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The only problem Innsbruck had, as always seems to be the case, was a lack of snow prior to the Olympics. The Austrian army was again enlisted, as in 1964, to pack the ski runs with snow hauled in from local mountaintops.  Fortunately, a huge storm one week before the Olympic solved the problem."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The competitions were well contested though no single athlete could be said to dominate, as in years past. " [:a {:href "/athletes/81850"} "Rosi Mittermaier"] " (FRG) was perhaps the most publicized. She won the downhill and slalom early in the Games and had a chance to equal the feats of " [:a {:href "/athletes/97080"} "Toni Sailer"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/87932"} "Jean-Claude Killy"] " by winning the giant slalom.  Older than many of the competitors, close to retirement, and born nearby, just across the German border at " [:a {:href "/place_names/6487"} "Reit im Winkl"] ", she was a heavy sentimental favorite. But it was not to be.  Canada’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/81567"} "Kathy Kreiner"] " defeated Rosi by only 12/100th’s of a second, but did not diminish her smile."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The most dramatic event was the hometown victory of Austria’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/88010"} "Franz Klammer"] " in the downhill. Klammer skiied on the edge throughout his run, to narrowly defeat Switzerland’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/97014"} "Bernhard Russi"] ". Some people wished to rename the " [:a {:href "/venues/57"} "Patscherkofel"] " course the Klammerkofel to honor his victory."] "\n\n" [:p {} "In figure skating, " [:a {:href "/athletes/82209"} "Irina Rodnina"] " (URS) again won a pairs gold medal, but with a different partner than in 1972. In men’s figure skating Britain’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/84944"} "John Curry"] " and Canada’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/84894"} "Toller Cranston"] " introduced a more balletic style than in years past. Among the women, " [:a {:href "/athletes/81238"} "Dorothy Hamill"] " of the United States became a camera favorite with her pixie looks, spright-like hairdo, and omnipresent squint, as she competed without her contact lenses. In winning the gold medal, she also proved she could skate more than a little bit, too."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Innsbruck succeeded. The Austrian town had overcome the problems that had beset Denver and the Olympic Winter idea and brought back sanity to the Winter Olympics. Although there were political problems in 1980 prior to the Winter Olympics, since 1976 no Olympic Winter city has had significant problems or controversies while hosting the Olympics.  Innsbruck saved the Olympic Winter Games."] "\n\n"] "\n\n" [:h2 {} "Bid process"] "\n" [:div {:class "description"} "\n" [:p {} "Bid voting at the 70th " [:a {:href "/meetings"} "IOC Session"] " in Amsterdam on 13 May 1970."] "\n\n" [:table {:class "table table-striped"} "\n  \t" [:tbody {} [:tr {} "\n  \t\t" [:th {}] "\n  \t\t" [:th {}] "\n  \t\t" [:th {} "Round 1"] "\n  \t\t" [:th {} "Round 2"] "\n  \t\t" [:th {} "Round 3"] "\n  \t"] [:tr {} "\n  \t\t" [:td {:class "city"} "Denver, Colorado"] "\n  \t\t" [:td {} "United States"] "\n  \t\t" [:td {} "29"] "\n  \t\t" [:td {} "29"] "\n  \t\t" [:td {} "39"] "\n  \t"] [:tr {} "\n  \t\t" [:td {:class "city"} "Sion"] "\n  \t\t" [:td {} "Switzerland"] "\n  \t\t" [:td {} "18"] "\n  \t\t" [:td {} "31"] "\n  \t\t" [:td {} "30"] "\n  \t"] [:tr {} "\n  \t\t" [:td {:class "city"} "Tampere"] "\n  \t\t" [:td {} "Finland"] "\n  \t\t" [:td {} "12"] "\n  \t\t" [:td {} "8"] "\n  \t\t" [:td {} "–"] "\n  \t"] [:tr {} "\n  \t\t" [:td {:class "city"} "Vancouver"] "\n  \t\t" [:td {} "Canada"] "\n  \t\t" [:td {} "9"] "\n  \t\t" [:td {} "–"] "\n  \t\t" [:td {} "–"] "\n  "]]] "\n\n" [:p {} "In November 1972, the citizens of Denver voted in a referendum to not host the Olympic Winter Games, fearing the negative environmental impact the Games could have. On 12 November 1972, Denver officially withdrew as host city. Bids were quickly solicited from Innsbruck (Austria), Lake Placid, New York (United States), Mont Blanc (France) and Tampere (Finland). Innsbruck was selected as the host at a meeting of the " [:a {:href "/definitions/83"} "IOC Executive Board"] " in Lausanne on 4 February 1973."] "\n\n"] "\n\n" [:h2 {} "Ceremonies"] "\n" [:table {:class "table table-striped table-condensed"} "\n" [:tbody {} [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} [:a {:href "/ceremonies/officially_opened"} "Officially opened by"]] "\n" [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/1100037"} "Rudolf Kirchschläger"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/AUT"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/AUT.png"}] "AUT"]] [:td {}] [:td {} "President Dr."] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} [:a {:href "/ceremonies/torch_bearers"} "Torchbearer"]] "\n" [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/1700513"} "Maria Moskholiou"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GRE"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GRE.png"}] "GRE"]] [:td {}] [:td {} "High Priestess at Olympia Flame Lighting Ceremony"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {}] "\n" [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/1700540"} "Spyros Tzavaras"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GRE"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GRE.png"}] "GRE"]] [:td {}] [:td {} "First torch-bearer at Olympia."] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {}] "\n" [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/85662"} "Josef Feistmantl"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/AUT"} [:im